# infra-utm-anon-egress secrets.
#
# Copy to .env and fill in, or just run `make preflight` -- it creates .env
# from this file with generated passwords at mode 0600. .env is gitignored.
#
# Everything here is OPTIONAL: an unset value means "do not configure that
# feature", never a broken build. Secrets reach Ansible through a 0600 temp
# vars file, never `-e key=value`, because command-line arguments are visible
# to every user on the host in `ps auxww`.
#
# --- What does NOT belong in this file, or anywhere else on disk -------------
#
# Your MULLVAD ACCOUNT NUMBER. Nothing here needs it: the build wants WireGuard
# configs, and those carry their own keys. It is a bearer credential with no
# password beside it and no way to rotate it -- whoever reads it controls the
# subscription -- so keeping it out of git is not enough. Keep it out of your
# shell history, terminal scrollback, and any tool that keeps a transcript.
#
# If yours has ever been exposed, generate a new account number and let the old
# one lapse. Note that deleting a local config file does not revoke a WireGuard
# key -- remove the key at Mullvad too.

# --- Console passwords ------------------------------------------------------
# Console/GUI login only. SSH stays key-only on both VMs (ssh_pwauth: false).
#
# cloud-init locks the user's password, which leaves the console able to show
# boot output but not accept a login. For the WORKSTATION that is the
# difference between having and not having a recovery path: it has no host port
# forward by design, so if the gateway or its own sshd fails, the UTM console is
# the only way in. The desktop greeter needs one too.
#
# Separate passwords per VM: compartmentalisation is the point of this repo,
# and the gateway is the box holding the VPN credentials.
GATEWAY_CONSOLE_PASSWORD=""
WORKSTATION_CONSOLE_PASSWORD=""

# --- Exit selection ---------------------------------------------------------
# Acquire the Mullvad account anonymously: Monero or cash, never a bank-linked
# method. The config is installed on the GATEWAY only and never reaches the
# workstation.
#
# Pick the exit by name and let the build assemble the multihop config, OR set
# MULLVAD_WG_CONFIG below to a file you assembled yourself. Setting both is an
# error rather than a precedence rule: a silently ignored key is exactly how
# you end up on an exit you did not choose. Leaving both empty is fine too --
# the gateway simply has no tunnel yet.
#
# MULLVAD_EXIT is the country the internet sees, at whatever precision:
#   "bg"             any Bulgarian server you have a config for
#   "bg-sof"         Sofia only
#   "bg-sof-wg-001"  exactly that server
# One config per server must exist in secrets/ as <hostname>.conf, downloaded
# from Mullvad's config generator (all countries at once is fine).
#
# Empty by default, because setting it makes `make preflight` fetch Mullvad's
# relay list over your real IP on the spot:
# MULLVAD_EXIT="bg"
MULLVAD_EXIT=""

# MULLVAD_ENTRY is the server the gateway actually connects to; same notation.
# Empty means any active server in a country OTHER than the exit's, which is
# the point of the second hop: the entry sees your real IP but not your
# destination, the exit sees your destination but not your IP. The build
# REFUSES to run if entry and exit share a country, or if the pair resolves to
# the single-hop port 51820.
MULLVAD_ENTRY=""

# The chosen pair is pinned in generated/mullvad-exit.chosen so `make configure`
# cannot move your tunnel to another country mid-lab. `make destroy` clears it;
# editing MULLVAD_EXIT discards it on the next run.

# Escape hatch: one hand-assembled config, used verbatim. Leave EMPTY when
# using MULLVAD_EXIT. This path skips the multihop enforcement -- you supplied
# the endpoint, so you own it -- and only warns if it is single-hop.
MULLVAD_WG_CONFIG=""
